Martin Read wrote on 08/04/2003 08:27 PM:
> In article <bgm6ht$c2u$01$1@news.t-online.com>,
> Stanislav Traykov  <stanislavNoeZpam@tortoises.org> wrote:
>>Richard Bos wrote on 08/04/2003 10:13 AM:
>>> Erm, no. Some compiles, at least, of Nethack use rand() if RANDOM is not
>>> #defined, and rand(), despite silly rumours, is not required to be a
>>> piss-poor version of a linear congruential RNG. It really all depends on
>>> which C library you use, and whether its writers have bothered to
>>> include a decent rand() - something they are certainly allowed to do.
>>
>>Indeed. I incorrectly assumed rand to be 32bit LCRNG as opposed to 48bit
>>*rand48. I guess this was/is(?) the case for many proprietary systems
>>which lead nethack to choose the *rand48 functions on UNIX systems.
>>
>>Perhaps some research should be done whether current systems generally
>>supply a decent rand. If this is so, there would be no reason to fall
>>back to *rand48 on systems with UNIX defined?
> 
> Linux does (because all current Linux installations use glibc), so one
> could certainly check for *that*.  I don't know about Solaris or the
> BSDs or the other proprietary unices.  Of course, on the BSDs, one could
> just use Berkeley random() from the system libc, which also works on
> Linux.

BSDs and Linux (man page says it conforms to BSD 4.3) seem to have it.
AIX and Solaris, too. On these it produces different results, but the
same as glibc on the Solaris. Probably because RAND_MAX is 32767 on the
AIX and Solaris (even with glibc). I don't know why this is so.. is this
RAND_MAX actually large enough? (Can't currently think of something
which requires a larger maximum)

Anyway the results of all these rand()'s are much better than those of
lrand48() regarding the limited set of subsequent results. [lrand48() is
uniform on all systems as the man pages state almost exactly how it is
calculated.]

